{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/python-rapidjson/python-rapidjson/issues/149"}}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf3 https://github.com/python-rapidjson/python-rapidjson/issues/149}}}\f0\fs22\par
\fs16 Hi, I'm developing [serializejson](https://github.com/SmartAudioTools/serializejson),  a python library for fast serialization and deserialization of python objects in JSON designed as a safe, interoperable and human-readable drop-in replacement for the Python [pickle](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html) package.\par
I use python-rapidjson in backend because it is the only one that is fast enough and has the sufficient API for serializejson needs. \par
Sadly python-rapidjson doesn't call default() method for tuples , time.struct_time, collections.counter, collections.ordereddict and collections.defaultdic. python-rapidjson serialize them as a list or dictionary without any possibility to catch them with default().\par
Did I miss something? would it be possible to add an option to call default for these objects?\par
Thanks ! \par
Baptiste \par
-------\par
    Did I miss something?\par
That seems correct: any iterable value is serialized as a JSON array.\par
    Would it be possible to add an option to call default for these objects?\par
Yes, but that's tricky: the default() function, when specified, is called for unrecognized values, and is expected to return something else that the library handles, simply recursing thru the serialization machinery. So, if we add an option to not recognize iterables, and thus passing them to the default(), what would it return?\par
------\par
