thanks for your response .\par
default() would return dictionnaries with "__class__" and "__init__" keys.\par
serializejson 0.1.0 is already able to load desired objects from such dictionnaries :\par
from serializejson import loads\par
print(loads('\{"__class__":"tuple","__init__":[1,2]\}'))\par
print(loads('\{"__class__":"time.struct_time","__init__":[2020,11,28,10,10,58,5,333,0]\}'))\par
print(loads('\{"__class__":"collections.Counter","__init__":\{"a":1,"b":2,"c":3\}\}'))\par
print(loads('\{"__class__":"collections.OrderedDict","__init__":\{"a":1,"b":2,"c":3\}\}'))\par
print(loads('\{"__class__":"collections.defaultdict","__init__":[null,\{"a":1,"b":2,"c":3\}]\}'))\par
The simplejson library is too slow but call default for all this objects (struct_time,Counter, OrderedDict, defaultdict) and for tuples if the option tuple_as_array is set False.\fs22\par
